    76  
    77  ### Crossover Analysis (10x slowdown threshold)
    78  
    79  | Input Type | Crossover Size | Ratio at Crossover |
    80  |------------|---------------|-------------------|
    81  | **sorted** | **256**       | 11.5x             |
    82  | random     | 16            | 12.2x             |
    83  | duplicates | 16            | 11.7x             |
    84  | **reverse**| **16**        | 27.3x             |
    85  
    86  ## Analysis
    87  
    88  ### Hypothesis validation
    89  
    90  The hypothesis predicted sorted would have the lowest crossover and reverse the
    91  highest. The actual results show:
    92  
    93  - **sorted**: crossover at size=256 (ratio=11.5x) — **LOWEST** ✓
    94  - **random**: crossover at size=16 (ratio=12.2x)
    95  - **duplicates**: crossover at size=16 (ratio=11.7x)
    96  - **reverse**: crossover at size=16 (ratio=27.3x) — **HIGHEST** ✓
    97  
    98  The hypothesis is **confirmed**: sorted input has the highest crossover size
    99  (256), while reverse, random, and duplicates all cross over at size=16.
   100  
   101  ### Key observations
   102  
   103  1. **Sorted input is dramatically more forgiving**: Insertion sort stays within
   104     10× of builtin_sorted up to size=256, while all other input types cross over
   105     at size=16. This is because sorted input is the best case for insertion sort
   106     (O(n) comparisons).
   107  
   108  2. **Three input types cross at the same size (16)**: random, duplicates, and
   109     reverse all cross at size=16, but with very different ratios:
   110     - random: 12.2x (just barely over threshold)
   111     - duplicates: 11.7x (just barely over threshold)
   112     - reverse: 27.3x (well over threshold)
   113  
   114  3. **The sorted crossover is "late"**: At size=128, sorted insertion sort is
   115     still only 9.96× slower — just under the 10× threshold. This is the largest
   116     size where insertion sort remains "comparable" to builtin_sorted.
   117  
   118  4. **Reverse-sorted is the worst case**: At size=1024, reverse-sorted insertion
   119     sort is 4702× slower than builtin_sorted. The gap grows quadratically.
   120  
   121  5. **Duplicates are not much better than random**: Despite having only 4 distinct
   122     values, duplicates cross at the same size as random (16) with a similar ratio
   123     (11.7x vs 12.2x). This suggests that the number of distinct values has less
   124     impact than the input order.
   125  
   126  ### Comparison with experiment_002 scaling results
   127  
   128  The crossover at size=256 for sorted input is consistent with the scaling
   129  analysis: sorted insertion sort scales as O(n) with ratios of ~3–5× between
   130  consecutive sizes. Starting from ~4.6× at size=16, the ratio grows to ~9.96×
   131  at size=128 and ~11.5× at size=256.
   132  
