+3|**Date**: 2025-10-14
+4|**Status**: Complete
+5|
+6|## Hypothesis
+7|
+8|For partially sorted inputs (where k% of elements are out of order), insertion
+9|sort will perform proportionally better than on random input, and the performance
+10|gap with `sorted()` will be smaller than on random input but larger than on fully
+11|sorted input. The gap should scale roughly linearly with the disorder percentage.
+12|
+13|## Design
+14|
+15|- **Sizes**: 32, 128, 512 (same as experiment_001)
+16|- **Disorder levels**: 0% (sorted), 10%, 25%, 50%, 75%, 100% (random)
+17|- **Disorder method**: Fisher-Yates shuffle of k% of elements in a sorted list
+18|  (swap k random pairs in a sorted array)
+19|- **Repetitions**: 50 per condition
+20|- **Timer**: time.perf_counter()
+21|- **Copying**: NOT timed — copy happens before timing starts
+22|- **Randomization**: measurement order randomized (seed 12345)
+23|- **Seeds**: disorder=42, sorted=1, random=42
+24|- **Correctness**: verified on every measured input before timing
+25|- **Python**: 3.13.15 / aarch64 Linux
